# Summary 

This archive provides a pre-configured, lightweight MinGW (Minimalist GNU for Windows) environment tailored for compiling and building C and C++ programs directly from the command line or via Makefile scripts.

## What is MinGW?
MinGW is a minimalist development environment for native Microsoft Windows applications. It provides a complete open-source programming toolchain that allows you to use standard GNU utilities on Windows.

### Included Toolchain (32-bit)
GCC / G++: The GNU C and C++ compilers required to translate your source code into executable binaries.
GNU Make: Build automation tool used to execute Makefile instructions and manage project dependencies efficiently.
Architecture: 32-bit version, ensuring high compatibility and lightweight performance for classroom environments.

## Installation Directory Structure
__To ensure everything works smoothly, extract the downloaded archive so that the folder structure looks exactly like this on your main drive:__

```C:\MinGW\bin\```

(Make sure the path to the executable binaries ends up being ```C:\MinGW\bin```).

Quick PATH Configuration (PowerShell)

The easiest way to make the compiler globally recognized by your system is to add it to your user PATH.

1. Copy the PowerShell script below to your clipboard:

    ```powershell
    $path = [System.Environment]::GetEnvironmentVariable("PATH", "User")
    if (-not ($path -like "*C:\mingw\bin*")) {
        [System.Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable("PATH", "C:\mingw\bin;$path", "User")
        Write-Host "Added C:\mingw\bin to PATH."
    } else {
        Write-Host "The path is already in PATH."
    }
    ```

2. Open PowerShell on your computer.

3. Paste the script and press Enter.

4. Restart your terminal or VS Code, and gcc / make will be fully ready to use!

---

# Summary 
Environment Setup & Build Basics
Welcome to the starter project! This is a minimalist C project designed to help you get familiar with Visual Studio Code, manual compilation, and basic build tools.

```
Project Structure
│   compile.bat        # Quick script for manual command-line compilation
│   main.c             # Simple starting C source code
│   Makefile           # Standard build automation script
│
└───.vscode
        launch.json    # Debugger configuration
        settings.json  # Editor workspace settings
        tasks.json     # Build tasks integration for VS Code
```

## Prerequisites (Required)

Before opening this project, ensure you have the following installed on your system:

- Visual Studio Code (VS Code) – Installed and ready.
- MinGW (GCC compiler for Windows)
- Crucial Step: Add the path to your MinGW bin directory (e.g., ```C:\MinGW\bin```) to your system's Environment Variables (PATH) so that gcc and make commands are globally accessible in your terminal.

## Getting Started:

- Download and extract the project archive (.zip) to your workspace folder.
- Open the extracted folder in Visual Studio Code.
- Explore the files and follow along with the lab instructions to test building via CLI, compile.bat, and the Makefile.